  "slug": "report-technical-4b00",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and one published study test how data pruning, sampling strategy, and structural context affect sequence model performance.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is learned sequence representations, mostly protein language models, applied where training data curation, sampling strategy, or structural context serves as a modeling lever. HyPLM-Flash, a 650M-parameter open protein language model trained on 726B tokens, reports matching ESM-2 and ESM C on ProteinGym and contact precision at lower cost. SeekRBP fuses protein language and structure embeddings and recasts negative sampling as a multi-armed bandit for receptor-binding protein identification. HyInv applies reinforcement learning and grouped draft-and-rewrite sampling within a 1.96M-parameter backbone-conditioned graph network for inverse folding. TriStageHLA uses LoRA-tuned ESM-2 for peptide pruning ahead of HLA binding and immunogenicity stages. Sequence Pruning is distinct in targeting DNA language-model pretraining, reporting up to 96% reduction in data processing. Evidence is largely preprint-based, and the benchmark comparisons are reported by the authors without independent validation here.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-16"
}
